## Q1:
what does it mean to be turing complete

## A1:
A system is Turing complete if it can simulate a universal Turing machine, meaning it has the power to run any logical or mathematical computation given enough time and memory. Key elements include conditional branching ("if" statements) and arbitrary/unlimited memory. [1, 2, 3] 
## Core Requirements

* Conditional Branching: The ability to change what the system does next based on data (like if/else choices).
* Loops or Recursion: The ability to repeat a set of rules over and over until a goal is met.
* Unlimited Memory: The ability to use more memory space as the problem gets larger (theoretical infinite tape). [1, 4] 

## Real-World Examples

* Programming Languages: Most popular languages like Python, C++, Java, and JavaScript are Turing complete.
* Unexpected Systems: Surprisingly, tools like Microsoft Excel formulas (without macros) and Redstone wiring in Minecraft can simulate a Turing machine. [5, 6] 

