## Q3:
Are there any programming lanaguges which is not turing complete?

## A3:
Yes, there are many programming and data languages that are intentionally not Turing complete. These are often called Total Functional programming languages or Domain-Specific Languages (DSLs). [1, 2, 3, 4] 
They are designed without unlimited loops or recursion, ensuring that every program written in them is guaranteed to finish running and never get stuck in an infinite loop. [5, 6, 7, 8, 9] 
## Examples of Turing Incomplete Languages

* SQL (Basic): Standard SQL queries (without recursive Common Table Expressions or loops) always finish processing data and stop.
* Regular Expressions (Regex): Standard regex engines scan text patterns using a fixed set of states and always terminate.
* Datalog: A subset of Prolog used in databases that lacks recursive functions, ensuring all queries are finite.
* Coq and Agda: Specialized languages used for mathematical proofs that forbid infinite loops to keep the proofs mathematically valid.
* Vyper: A smart contract language for the Ethereum blockchain designed to be non-Turing complete to easily calculate maximum gas costs.
* JSON / HTML / CSS (Standard): These are data or styling languages that cannot perform general logic or math computations on their own. [10, 11, 12, 13, 14] 

## Why Make a Language Incomplete?

* Guaranteed Halting: The program will never freeze, crash, or loop forever.
* Security: It prevents malicious actors from launching Denial of Service (DoS) attacks via infinite loops.
* Predictability: Computers can analyze the code ahead of time to calculate exactly how much time or memory it will need. [15, 16] 

