## Q4:
If you can make quine using programming lanagues it means it is turing complete, else the language is not turing complete?

## A4:
No, the ability to write a quine does not mean a language is Turing complete, and some Turing-incomplete languages can still produce quines. [1, 2, 3] 
## Why the Link Doesn't Hold

* Quines only require self-replication: A quine is a program that outputs its own source code. This requires a way to handle strings and output data, but it does not require complex logic, infinite loops, or arbitrary memory. [4, 5, 6, 7, 8] 
* Turing completeness requires general computation: To be Turing complete, a language must be able to solve any computable problem (like simulating a physics engine or running a web server). A language can be smart enough to print itself while being too simple to do general math or loops. [9, 10, 11, 12, 13] 

## Examples that Break the Rule

* Turing Incomplete but makes a Quine: HTML is a data language, not a Turing-complete programming language. However, a single blank HTML file or an empty text file outputs exactly nothing—which matches its own source code (a trivial quine). More advanced, strictly finite functional languages can also easily print their own structure without needing loops. [14, 15, 16, 17, 18] 
* The Mathematical Proof (Kleene's Recursion Theorem): Computer science theory proves that any language capable of basic string manipulation can form a quine, regardless of whether it can run complex, infinite algorithms. [19] 

