## Q5:
But what we want to make a quine which replicates forever, in this case a non turing compete lanaguage won't be able to do so
## A5:
An infinitely replicating quine is a sequence of programs where each one outputs the next code in the chain forever, or a single program that continuously streams its own code to the screen without stopping.
You are entirely correct that a strictly total language (one that guarantees every program must halt) cannot do this. However, a language does not need to be Turing complete to run an infinite loop. [1] 
## The Missing Piece: The "Looping" In-Between
Turing completeness requires both infinite looping and arbitrary memory manipulation. A language can be restricted so that it can loop forever, but still be completely unable to perform general calculations. [2, 3, 4, 5] 

* Example 1: A Repeating GIF or Video. A video file loops its frames forever. It uses infinite repetition, but it is not a programming language and cannot solve math problems.
* Example 2: A Finite State Machine. Imagine a simple machine with a single instruction: PRINT "A" -> GOTO LINE 1. It will loop forever printing "A", but it has zero memory and cannot simulate a computer.

## The Rule for an Infinite Quine
To make a quine that replicates forever, a language only needs two features:

   1. String Manipulation: To format and print its own code.
   2. An Infinite Loop Primitive: A way to repeat the output action forever. [6, 7, 8, 9] 

If a language has these two features but lacks variables, memory storage, or conditional logic (if/else statements), it is not Turing complete, but it can still run your infinitely replicating quine. [10, 11, 12] 
