## Q6:
what about if we want to perform conways way of life?

## A6:
Conway's Game of Life is a cellular automaton governed by simple rules, but it is actually Turing complete on its own. [1, 2, 3, 4] 
To implement Conway's Game of Life, a programming language must be Turing complete. [5] 
## Why Game of Life Requires Turing Completeness
To run the Game of Life, a language must handle three specific computational demands:

* Infinite Grid (Arbitrary Memory): The game board can grow infinitely in any direction. The language needs to allocate and manage unpredictable amounts of memory as cells multiply. [6] 
* Conditional Logic (The Rules): The language must evaluate conditions for every cell (e.g., if a live cell has fewer than two neighbors, it dies; if a dead cell has exactly three neighbors, it becomes alive). [7, 8, 9] 
* Infinite Looping (Generations): The simulation must loop through generations continuously without a predetermined stopping point. [10] 

Because Turing-incomplete languages (like standard SQL or basic Regex) lack arbitrary memory tracking or infinite loops, they cannot simulate an unbounded Game of Life.
## The Reverse is Also True
Because Conway's Game of Life is Turing complete, you can build moving patterns inside the game (like "gliders" and "glider guns") to act as logic gates, wires, and memory registers. People have actually used the Game of Life to build working calculators, digital clocks, and even the Game of Life running inside the Game of Life. [11, 12, 13, 14, 15] 
