## Q7:
What does it mean when u say "Standard SQL"
## A7:
When someone says "Standard SQL," they are referring to the official, universally agreed-upon blueprint for the SQL language established by the [American National Standards Institute (ANSI)](https://www.dbvis.com/thetable/standard-ansi-sql-what-it-is-and-why-it-matters/) and the [International Organization for Standardization (ISO)](https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/19/sqlrf/SQL-Standards.html). [1, 2] 
It acts as a common core language designed to ensure that basic database commands work the exact same way, whether you are using MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, Microsoft SQL Server, or BigQuery. [2] 
------------------------------
## 1. The Core Components
Standard SQL outlines the essential rules for querying and structuring data. If you stick strictly to the standard, commands like these should theoretically run on any relational database system: [2, 3, 4] 

* Data Retrieval: SELECT, FROM, WHERE, JOIN, GROUP BY
* Data Modification: INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE
* Structure Management: CREATE TABLE, DROP TABLE [1, 2, 3, 5, 6] 

## 2. Standard SQL vs. Dialects
While the standard exists, virtually no database company adheres to it 100% perfectly. Instead, every database platform adopts the "Standard SQL" core and adds its own unique flavor, custom tools, and extra performance features. These are called dialects: [3, 7, 8, 9, 10] 

| Platform | Dialect / Extension Name | What it Adds |
|---|---|---|
| Microsoft SQL Server | T-SQL (Transact-SQL) | Custom variable handling, error trapping, and row processing. |
| Oracle Database | PL/SQL (Procedural Language) | Integrates core procedural programming structures (like loops). |
| PostgreSQL | PL/pgSQL | Advanced data types and customizable server functions. |

## 3. The "Turing Complete" Context
In our previous discussion, basic "Standard SQL" was mentioned as a classic example of a Turing-incomplete language. [11] 
When database engineers talk about the oldest versions of Standard SQL (like the famous SQL-92 standard), they mean a strictly bounded language. It was built purely to filter and fetch rows. Because it had no loop mechanisms, it was mathematically guaranteed to finish running and never freeze the database server. [5, 11, 12, 13] 
(Note: Modern versions of the SQL standard have added complex features like Recursive Common Table Expressions and Persistent Stored Modules, making modern SQL variants Turing complete in practice). [11, 12] 
## 4. Google BigQuery Exception
If you are working with data analytics, you might hear the phrase "Standard SQL" used in a specific way by Google Cloud. Google BigQuery originally used a custom, highly restricted language called "Legacy SQL". They later updated their platform to support the global industry conventions, naming their updated engine "Standard SQL" to show it complies with modern ANSI/ISO standards. [14, 15, 16, 17, 18] 
