## Q8:
I am a bit confused about ruby in this case. It does have infinite loops so it must be turing complete but at the same time it also have DSL which are supposed to be turing incomplete
## A8:
That confusion is completely natural! The mix-up happens because we are blending Ruby the programming language with Ruby the tool used to build DSLs.
Ruby itself is 100% Turing complete. The fact that you can build Domain-Specific Languages (DSLs) in Ruby does not change that. [1] 
Here is how those two pieces fit together without contradicting each other.
## 1. Ruby is an "Internal" DSL Engine
There are two types of DSLs:

* External DSLs: Written in a completely separate language with its own custom parser (like standard SQL or regular expressions). These are often Turing incomplete.
* Internal DSLs (Embedded DSLs): Written entirely inside an existing programming language. [2, 3] 

Ruby is famous for Internal DSLs. When you look at a Ruby DSL (like a Chef recipe, a Rakefile, or a Ruby on Rails routing file), you aren't looking at a new language. You are looking at regular, valid Ruby code written using clever syntax tricks (like omitting parentheses and using blocks) to look like a specialized language. [4, 5, 6] 
## 2. Ruby DSLs Inherit Ruby's Power
Because a Ruby DSL is just disguised Ruby code, the DSL itself is actually Turing complete. [7] 
For example, look at this route file from a Ruby on Rails web app. It looks like a simple configuration file (a DSL):

Rails.application.routes.draw do
  get '/welcome', to: 'pages#home'end

However, because this is just Ruby, you can inject an infinite loop or complex math right into the middle of your configuration if you want to:

Rails.application.routes.draw do
  # This is still a Rails route file, but it's also pure Ruby!
  while true
    puts "I am looping forever inside a DSL!"
  end
  
  get '/welcome', to: 'pages#home'end

The example above proves that Ruby DSLs are generally Turing complete because they have full access to Ruby's underlying features (loops, recursion, variables). [8, 9, 10] 
## 3. Can you make a Turing-Incomplete DSL in Ruby?
Yes, but you have to do it on purpose by restricting the environment.
If a Ruby developer wants to create a truly safe, Turing-incomplete DSL for users to type into a system, they cannot just let the user run raw Ruby code. They have to use a tool like a sandbox or a secure interpreter (like the Liquid templating language, which was built in Ruby but strips out loops and dangerous logic).
## Summary

* Ruby is Turing complete because it has infinite loops and arbitrary memory.
* Ruby DSLs are just a stylized way of writing Ruby, meaning they are also Turing complete by default.
* Not all DSLs are Turing incomplete; only External DSLs (or heavily sandboxed Internal DSLs) guarantee that a program will always halt. [11, 12, 13, 14] 

